** WARNING OF SUN 68K/SPARC COMPATIBILITY PROBLEM **

I have nethack running here on 68k and Sparc based suns. I compiled PL8
on the Sparc and then on the 68k leaving special levels produced by a
68k lev_comp. Now, while playing the game (one of my best games ever
--- isn't it always :-) I got teleported from level 10 to level 30
where the program died in disorder as it couldn't create the drawbridge
etc.

The problem is that the 68k cc pads structs out to an even number of
bytes (i.e. an exact number of 16 bit worrds) while the Sparc compiler
doesn't. Hence when reading in a 68k file of data written as structs
on a Sparc machine (or vice versa) chaos ensues. Exactly the same
problem will occur if you tried to read such a file on another machine
with a different byte-sex (e.g. a VAX).

The solution is to rewrite the maze writing and read routines in
lev_comp.c and spl_lev.c so that each element of each struct is written
out explicitly using a routine which outputs the item byte by byte ---
this is the mechanism used in TeX to maintain compatibility of its data
files across varying systems.

I fear the same will apply to the routines that read and write bones
levels.

There is a quick and tacky hack -- add

#ifdef sparc
xchar padding;
#endif

to each struct with an odd number of bytes in sp_lev.h. This will solve
things now, but I think correct patch is required! Pity I didn't fully
appreciate what was going on before PL8 as I have had this bug before
but thought there was another cause.

